WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.208 --> 00:00:10.443
the small RNA revolution that occurred

00:00:10.444 --> 00:00:15.748
between 1990 and today, and how that emerged.

00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:17.016
My name is Gary Ruvkun.

00:00:17.017 --> 00:00:20.520
I'm a professor of genetics are Harvard Medical School.

00:00:20.521 --> 00:00:22.421
One learns as an undergraduate,

00:00:22.422 --> 00:00:25.458
the central dogma of molecular biology,

00:00:25.459 --> 00:00:27.860
where DNA makes RNA, that makes proteins.

00:00:27.861 --> 00:00:32.865
And small RNAs have modified that over the last 20 years.

00:00:32.866 --> 00:00:36.335
The first example of a modification was the microRNAs,

00:00:36.336 --> 00:00:37.770
that I will go into great detail today,

00:00:37.771 --> 00:00:39.405
and they are thought to be made

00:00:39.406 --> 00:00:43.776
from very small transcripts, 22 nucleotides long,

00:00:43.777 --> 00:00:47.980
that then regulate the translation of target mRNAs,

00:00:47.981 --> 00:00:51.050
to then cause less protein products.

00:00:51.051 --> 00:00:52.985
So it's another layer of regulation.

00:00:52.986 --> 00:00:56.689
But in addition to microRNAs, there are also

00:00:56.690 --> 00:01:00.193
many other tiny RNAs that are made inside of cells

00:01:00.194 --> 00:01:06.365
that probably regulate the production of RNA from genes

00:01:06.366 --> 00:01:08.768
and also add a regulatory locus.

00:01:08.769 --> 00:01:10.636
And those were all discovered at about the same time,

00:01:10.637 --> 00:01:11.871
and I'll talk about that, too.

00:01:11.872 --> 00:01:15.808
So the star of the show is C. elegans, the nematode.

00:01:15.809 --> 00:01:20.112
And this is the animal that is not as big as my arm,

00:01:20.113 --> 00:01:24.884
it is is a millimeter long and it has been

00:01:24.885 --> 00:01:29.755
a subject of thousands of researchers worldwide who work on it.

00:01:29.756 --> 00:01:34.794
It started as a field by Sydney Brenner in the early 60s,

00:01:34.795 --> 00:01:37.864
as the most simple complicated animal to work on.

00:01:37.865 --> 00:01:44.237
The worm is an animal and it's phylogenetically related

00:01:44.238 --> 00:01:48.074
to us up here, but pretty distant.

00:01:48.075 --> 00:01:50.943
And much simplified as an animal.

00:01:50.944 --> 00:01:55.548
So simple that instead of having billions of cells,

00:01:55.549 --> 00:01:58.751
it has 959 cells as a full grown adult.

00:01:58.752 --> 00:02:00.920
This is the lineage of the animal,

00:02:00.921 --> 00:02:04.457
it was worked out by John Sulston in the early 70s.

00:02:04.458 --> 00:02:06.826
This is showing the pattern of divisions

00:02:06.827 --> 00:02:08.294
that take place in the embryo.

00:02:08.295 --> 00:02:11.631
These are the divisions that take place during larval stage 1,

00:02:11.632 --> 00:02:14.667
as you can see here, larval stage 2,

00:02:14.668 --> 00:02:18.404
larval stage 3, larval stage 4, and out pops an adult.

00:02:18.405 --> 00:02:21.641
It produces progeny every 3 days,

00:02:21.642 --> 00:02:24.610
so it's a very fast genetic system

00:02:24.611 --> 00:02:26.045
that attracted a lot of talent.

00:02:26.046 --> 00:02:28.915
And the field of C. elegans has been

00:02:28.916 --> 00:02:31.450
very influential to me, in particular.

00:02:31.451 --> 00:02:35.388
It's just populated with really wonderful people

00:02:35.389 --> 00:02:37.023
who work on many different biological problems.

00:02:37.024 --> 00:02:39.825
And I've always felt blessed to have been

00:02:39.826 --> 00:02:43.362
surrounded by people who work on

00:02:43.363 --> 00:02:46.332
so many different aspects of the biology of this animal.

00:02:46.333 --> 00:02:49.135
It's a way to learn everything about biology

00:02:49.136 --> 00:02:50.903
in a microcosm on one organism.

00:02:50.904 --> 00:02:56.876
The particular project that I'll be describing in detail

00:02:56.877 --> 00:03:00.646
was started at the MRC,

00:03:00.647 --> 00:03:02.682
which is the center of Medical Research Council,

00:03:02.683 --> 00:03:05.351
which was the center where worms started in Sydney Brenner's lab.

00:03:05.352 --> 00:03:07.854
Actually Marty Chalfie started this project,

00:03:07.855 --> 00:03:14.594
discovering the lin-4 mutation, back in the mid-70s.

00:03:14.595 --> 00:03:18.264
And this is the mutation that causes lineage changes

00:03:18.265 --> 00:03:22.101
and those lineage changes look like changes in time.

00:03:22.102 --> 00:03:24.937
So if you look at the wildtype lineage over here,

00:03:24.938 --> 00:03:28.541
this complicated pattern of divisions that takes place

00:03:28.542 --> 00:03:32.445
in the larval stage 1, only takes place in larval stage 1

00:03:32.446 --> 00:03:38.384
in wild type, yet in the lin-4, it goes at the L1, L2, L3, and L4 stage.

00:03:38.385 --> 00:03:42.755
It keeps reiterating, so this animal is retarded

00:03:42.756 --> 00:03:45.224
and never really gets to the adult stage.

00:03:45.225 --> 00:03:48.461
It always has patterns of division as if

00:03:48.462 --> 00:03:50.129
it's a larval animal.

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:54.600
And what really sort of launched this project

00:03:54.601 --> 00:03:57.703
to be really important to do,

00:03:57.704 --> 00:04:01.507
was when Victor Ambros began to work on it

00:04:01.508 --> 00:04:06.012
when he was a postdoc in Bob Horvitz's lab in the early 80s,

00:04:06.013 --> 00:04:09.081
and discovered that gene lin-14 could suppress

00:04:09.082 --> 00:04:10.983
the mutations in lin-4.

00:04:10.984 --> 00:04:13.452
So it could take this complicated set of divisions

00:04:13.453 --> 00:04:17.023
that are temporally abhorrent,

00:04:17.024 --> 00:04:20.459
and instead of doing L1, L1, L1,

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:22.195
it would go straight to L2,

00:04:22.196 --> 00:04:26.032
and therefore have a pattern of divisions

00:04:26.033 --> 00:04:27.667
that's distinct in time.

00:04:27.668 --> 00:04:31.537
And this allowed him to infer that

00:04:31.538 --> 00:04:35.274
lin-4 was the negative regulator of the gene lin-14.

00:04:35.275 --> 00:04:39.111
To set up how this developmental timing works,

00:04:39.112 --> 00:04:43.583
And so Victor and I worked together as postdocs on this project,

00:04:43.584 --> 00:04:47.053
to try and figure out how lin-4 and lin-14 regulate

00:04:47.054 --> 00:04:51.958
former regulatory axis to set up the timing of the animal.

00:04:51.959 --> 00:04:57.763
So we together identified the genetic locus

00:04:57.764 --> 00:05:01.767
the actual molecular basis of lin-14

00:05:01.768 --> 00:05:04.370
and the first thing we figured out

00:05:04.371 --> 00:05:08.741
was that mutations that activate the lin-14 gene

00:05:08.742 --> 00:05:12.578
are deletions not of the open reading frame of the gene here,

00:05:12.579 --> 00:05:14.313
it's downstream of the open reading frame,

00:05:14.314 --> 00:05:17.750
but actually mutations in the 3' untranslated region

00:05:17.751 --> 00:05:21.187
or translocation of the 3' untranslated region,

00:05:21.188 --> 00:05:23.623
that takes these conserved regions shown in gray

00:05:23.624 --> 00:05:28.628
and either deletes them or translocates them away.

00:05:28.629 --> 00:05:33.633
So that said that there's an off-switch on the lin-14 gene

00:05:33.634 --> 00:05:35.501
and this is part of the off-switch.

00:05:35.502 --> 00:05:37.803
And the way it's turned off,

00:05:37.804 --> 00:05:42.241
is by this negative regulator, lin-4, shown here.

00:05:42.242 --> 00:05:44.477
And so what was lin-4?

00:05:44.478 --> 00:05:46.779
The key breakthrough here came out of Victor's lab

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:50.249
when he was an assistant professor at Harvard,

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:52.118
and what his lab figured out was

00:05:52.119 --> 00:05:55.188
Rosalind Lee, and Rhonda Feinbaum, and Victor

00:05:55.189 --> 00:05:58.257
was that lin-4 didn't code for a protein at all,

00:05:58.258 --> 00:06:03.062
it codes for very small RNA that's 22 nucleotides long

00:06:03.063 --> 00:06:04.664
that's shown in the gray box here,

00:06:04.665 --> 00:06:10.069
the precursor for lin-4 is shown as this double stranded RNA.

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:15.808
And this RNA is upregulated right at the time of L1

00:06:15.809 --> 00:06:19.412
which fits with the phenotype of what we were studying.

00:06:19.413 --> 00:06:22.815
So after the Ambros lab figured out that lin-4

00:06:22.816 --> 00:06:25.284
encodes a tiny RNA, we, Victor and I,

00:06:25.285 --> 00:06:29.188
compared the sequence of his lin-4,

00:06:29.189 --> 00:06:31.123
shown on the bottom strand here,

00:06:31.124 --> 00:06:32.725
to the sequence of lin-14,

00:06:32.726 --> 00:06:36.162
assuming it might be a direct interaction,

00:06:36.163 --> 00:06:38.598
which you never know if it's direct or indirect in biology,

00:06:38.599 --> 00:06:40.099
but let's assume simplicity.

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:43.135
And so we could immediately see

00:06:43.136 --> 00:06:46.939
that there were 7 different sites on the lin-14 UTR,

00:06:46.940 --> 00:06:48.774
so they're numbered 1-7 here,

00:06:48.775 --> 00:06:52.011
and for example, here's number 1,

00:06:52.012 --> 00:06:54.580
and that's shown over here,

00:06:54.581 --> 00:06:59.051
the top strand is lin-14, the bottom strand is lin-4.

00:06:59.052 --> 00:07:02.555
And in every case, there was a duplex,

00:07:02.556 --> 00:07:04.423
but as you notice, it's not a perfect duplex.

00:07:04.424 --> 00:07:06.425
The lin-4 would bulge a C here,

00:07:06.426 --> 00:07:09.762
it'll bulge an ACCUCA over here,

00:07:09.763 --> 00:07:14.834
and each one had a sort of non-perfect duplex.

00:07:14.835 --> 00:07:18.738
On the other hand, we could see by evolutionary comparisons,

00:07:18.739 --> 00:07:22.642
comparing the sequence of lin-14 on the top strand

00:07:22.643 --> 00:07:26.179
to between Caenorhabditis elegans and briggsae,

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:29.348
that all the bolded letters are conserved

00:07:29.349 --> 00:07:31.184
between C. elegans and C. briggsae.

00:07:31.185 --> 00:07:34.654
So that said the sequence of this is important enough

00:07:34.655 --> 00:07:36.889
for the animal to have conserved it over many

00:07:36.890 --> 00:07:38.090
billions of years.

00:07:38.091 --> 00:07:43.529
And so we proved that these elements were important

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:47.500
by moving this 3' UTR into reporter genes

00:07:47.501 --> 00:07:51.003
and knocking out these sites to prove it was important.

00:07:51.004 --> 00:07:55.942
And this was the first example

00:07:55.943 --> 00:07:59.645
of a regulatory RNA that was that small

00:07:59.646 --> 00:08:01.080
in the case of lin-4.

00:08:01.081 --> 00:08:05.184
And it was much smaller than any other regulatory RNA,

00:08:05.185 --> 00:08:08.487
for example the uRNAs are more like 100 nucleotides,

00:08:08.488 --> 00:08:09.856
than 20 nucleotides.

00:08:09.857 --> 00:08:15.394
In this developmental biology community,

00:08:15.395 --> 00:08:16.929
the community that we were a part of,

00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:21.033
that included, for example, many C. elegans geneticists,

00:08:21.034 --> 00:08:24.537
drosophila geneticists, they paid attention to this.

00:08:24.538 --> 00:08:26.906
We definitely talked about this at Gordon conferences

00:08:26.907 --> 00:08:27.840
and things like that.

00:08:27.841 --> 00:08:30.176
But there was always a view that this was

00:08:30.177 --> 00:08:33.779
kind of a quirky thing, that may be a quirk

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:35.882
of the lineage of C. elegans.

00:08:35.883 --> 00:08:40.019
Because remember, we're dealing with a lineage here

00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:43.923
which was a distinct way to think about developmental biology.

00:08:43.924 --> 00:08:48.694
And really sort of marginal in comparison

00:08:48.695 --> 00:08:51.697
to how the wider developmental biology community thought

00:08:51.698 --> 00:08:54.867
more about tissue interactions and cell interactions.

00:08:54.868 --> 00:08:59.338
And so, yes, we could present it at meetings and things like that,

00:08:59.339 --> 00:09:02.041
but it wasn't really, there wasn't a stampede

00:09:02.042 --> 00:09:04.377
for people to start work on it, that's for sure.

00:09:04.378 --> 00:09:07.713
On the other hand, the regulatory RNA community

00:09:07.714 --> 00:09:09.615
the people who work on the ribosome,

00:09:09.616 --> 00:09:11.651
how the tRNAs bind acceptor sites,

00:09:11.652 --> 00:09:13.986
that sort of thing, splicing,

00:09:13.987 --> 00:09:16.022
they were totally enthralled.

00:09:16.023 --> 00:09:19.792
So it was exciting to us,

00:09:19.793 --> 00:09:23.763
to be thrust into the regulatory RNA community.

00:09:23.764 --> 00:09:25.998
Remember, we're coming as developmental biologists,

00:09:25.999 --> 00:09:30.069
what we happened to find was a regulatory RNA

00:09:30.070 --> 00:09:33.606
and it's target, thrust us into a new ecosystem.

00:09:33.607 --> 00:09:37.476
And that ecosystem informed us in many ways,

00:09:37.477 --> 00:09:39.645
that it's very sophisticated.

00:09:39.646 --> 00:09:41.848
How the ribosomal RNA folds,

00:09:41.849 --> 00:09:43.783
how the ribosome works,

00:09:43.784 --> 00:09:46.853
these are inspirational fields.

00:09:46.854 --> 00:09:50.389
And it's one of the great things about biology,

00:09:50.390 --> 00:09:53.559
there are these islands of specialization,

00:09:53.560 --> 00:09:59.198
and you can learn a lot from each island.

00:09:59.199 --> 00:10:03.636
Biology teaches us that the islands are where

00:10:03.637 --> 00:10:06.739
the most extreme variation occurs.

00:10:06.740 --> 00:10:11.511
So how does the lin-4 microRNA work?

00:10:11.512 --> 00:10:15.381
Well, lin-4 gets upregulated in the L1 stage

00:10:15.382 --> 00:10:18.518
and that causes less production of the lin-14 protein,

00:10:18.519 --> 00:10:22.355
that's its target, but it doesn't really affect the mRNA levels

00:10:22.356 --> 00:10:24.490
nearly as much as it affects the protein levels.

00:10:24.491 --> 00:10:26.926
So that said that the way that microRNAs work

00:10:26.927 --> 00:10:31.731
is that one microRNA is by regulating the transcription of target proteins.

00:10:31.732 --> 00:10:34.433
We still don't know, even 20 years later,

00:10:34.434 --> 00:10:36.169
exactly how that's working.

00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:42.375
It's still an object of much research in microRNAs.

00:10:42.376 --> 00:10:48.080
The next stage, right now we're at 1993,

00:10:48.081 --> 00:10:50.883
approximately the next stage is 7 years later,

00:10:50.884 --> 00:10:54.587
when we start doing genetics to try and fill out the rest

00:10:54.588 --> 00:10:57.757
of the pathway. To figure out who else is being regulated.

00:10:57.758 --> 00:11:00.226
To specify these lineages, and we get let-7

00:11:00.227 --> 00:11:03.863
as a suppressor of a weak lin-14 mutation.

00:11:03.864 --> 00:11:06.632
And when we figure out what let-7 is,

00:11:06.633 --> 00:11:09.802
we figure out that it's another microRNA.

00:11:09.803 --> 00:11:12.638
In this case, looks a lot like the first one,

00:11:12.639 --> 00:11:14.674
it's not homologous at all, but it's analogous.

00:11:14.675 --> 00:11:16.375
It has the same stem loop structure,

00:11:16.376 --> 00:11:20.379
the gray box is the 22 nucleotide product of that.

00:11:20.380 --> 00:11:25.484
And in this case, the let-7 microRNA,

00:11:25.485 --> 00:11:29.589
when we compared it's sequence across emerging databases,

00:11:29.590 --> 00:11:31.457
now remember, this is the year 2000,

00:11:31.458 --> 00:11:34.360
as opposed to 1992, so genome databases

00:11:34.361 --> 00:11:36.429
are much more sophisticated.

00:11:36.430 --> 00:11:39.999
The human genome in the year 2000 is about 30% done,

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:43.436
so we can compare it to the 30% done genome sequence,

00:11:43.437 --> 00:11:46.038
not by doing northern blots of anything like that,

00:11:46.039 --> 00:11:48.708
but by just doing a BLASTn analysis,

00:11:48.709 --> 00:11:51.043
just asking "is the nucleotide conserved?"

00:11:51.044 --> 00:11:54.080
And boom! We could see it 20 seconds later.

00:11:54.081 --> 00:11:57.049
And that was an amazing moment,

00:11:57.050 --> 00:12:01.220
because the activation energy to actually do

00:12:01.221 --> 00:12:03.723
a northern blot or some kind of experiment

00:12:03.724 --> 00:12:07.226
to look for conservation takes some oomph to do that.

00:12:07.227 --> 00:12:10.096
But looking at a genome, you could do it in 20 seconds

00:12:10.097 --> 00:12:11.063
and know the answer.

00:12:11.064 --> 00:12:15.168
And so we sort of instantly knew that let-7 was conserved

00:12:15.169 --> 00:12:19.305
in flies, in humans, in genome sequences,

00:12:19.306 --> 00:12:22.475
but we also had to of course prove that it was a small RNA.

00:12:22.476 --> 00:12:24.110
So we did do a lot of northern blots

00:12:24.111 --> 00:12:26.112
to a whole zoo of creatures.

00:12:26.113 --> 00:12:32.685
And that was very influenced by comparative

00:12:32.686 --> 00:12:36.022
developmental biology conferences,

00:12:36.023 --> 00:12:39.292
that Eric Davidson had run at the Marine Biological Labs,

00:12:39.293 --> 00:12:42.028
which is where I'm actually speaking to this camera right now.

00:12:42.029 --> 00:12:44.964
Now at about the same time,

00:12:44.965 --> 00:12:46.966
that we were finding this second microRNA,

00:12:46.967 --> 00:12:48.534
in the year 2000.

00:12:48.535 --> 00:12:52.672
Fire and Mello, and Baulcombe and Hamilton,

00:12:52.673 --> 00:12:58.411
had found that tiny RNAs are produced in RNA interference,

00:12:58.412 --> 00:13:01.447
which was a very hot topic at that point.

00:13:01.448 --> 00:13:04.851
It was very mysterious, how injecting double stranded RNAs

00:13:04.852 --> 00:13:06.619
into organisms would inactivate genes.

00:13:06.620 --> 00:13:10.356
Hamilton and Baulcombe found 22 nucleotide RNAs.

00:13:10.357 --> 00:13:15.461
I was enthralled by the fact that the microRNAs

00:13:15.462 --> 00:13:19.332
and the siRNAs are the same size.

00:13:19.333 --> 00:13:23.536
I remember the number 22 was an important number

00:13:23.537 --> 00:13:26.105
in the Kabbalistic numerology,

00:13:26.106 --> 00:13:27.707
and sort of as a joke,

00:13:27.708 --> 00:13:29.742
I found websites to show my lab,

00:13:29.743 --> 00:13:31.911
and they worried about me doing this.

00:13:31.912 --> 00:13:35.715
Much more productive was to say,

00:13:35.716 --> 00:13:40.386
okay, if they produce the same size RNAs,

00:13:40.387 --> 00:13:42.355
maybe they use some of the same enzymology.

00:13:42.356 --> 00:13:46.626
And the enzymes that take double stranded RNAs

00:13:46.627 --> 00:13:49.161
and chop them into siRNAs were being discovered.

00:13:49.162 --> 00:13:51.764
For example, by Greg Hannon's lab at Cold Spring Harbor,

00:13:51.765 --> 00:13:55.134
and so we could do the experiment of inactivating

00:13:55.135 --> 00:13:59.205
the gene for that double-stranded RNA, Dicer,

00:13:59.206 --> 00:14:02.208
RNase Dicer, and show that was actually important

00:14:02.209 --> 00:14:05.444
in how microRNAs get processed as well.

00:14:05.445 --> 00:14:10.283
The other importance of the synchrony

00:14:10.284 --> 00:14:13.886
of the discoveries of the RNAi pathway and the micro pathway

00:14:13.887 --> 00:14:19.358
is that there was a huge interest in RNAi as a tool.

00:14:19.359 --> 00:14:23.296
The C. elegans community was probably one of the very first

00:14:23.297 --> 00:14:26.532
genetic communities to start using RNAi in a big way.

00:14:26.533 --> 00:14:29.569
But it also started to happen a lot in plants,

00:14:29.570 --> 00:14:32.972
and once siRNAs were discovered in animals,

00:14:32.973 --> 00:14:36.008
they started to be used all over animal labs.

00:14:36.009 --> 00:14:40.446
So the interest in it as a tool was immense,

00:14:40.447 --> 00:14:44.450
and it made the interest in microRNAs

00:14:44.451 --> 00:14:46.953
and small RNAs in general, much larger

00:14:46.954 --> 00:14:49.188
than if it was just microRNAs.

00:14:49.189 --> 00:14:54.327
So after let-7 was discovered as a conserved microRNA,

00:14:54.328 --> 00:14:58.364
there was a concerted effort to purify by cloning

00:14:58.365 --> 00:15:00.099
many different microRNAs.

00:15:00.100 --> 00:15:02.969
And actually, Victor Ambros's lab was one

00:15:02.970 --> 00:15:05.371
of the first to do that, along with the Bartel lab

00:15:05.372 --> 00:15:06.272
and the Tuschl lab.

00:15:06.273 --> 00:15:08.774
And many microRNAs were discovered,

00:15:08.775 --> 00:15:10.710
and within a year of two, there were thousands

00:15:10.711 --> 00:15:14.213
of microRNAs, the current census must be

00:15:14.214 --> 00:15:16.115
tens of thousands of microRNAs.

00:15:16.116 --> 00:15:20.820
And it is known now that microRNAs regulate many many

00:15:20.821 --> 00:15:24.357
different targets, and are regulating those targets

00:15:24.358 --> 00:15:27.593
to regulate all kinds of processes.

00:15:27.594 --> 00:15:30.062
They work everywhere from floral development

00:15:30.063 --> 00:15:32.765
in plants to perhaps how memories are formed.

00:15:32.766 --> 00:15:37.170
There are thousands and thousands of papers on microRNAs

00:15:37.171 --> 00:15:40.640
in all different fields, from plants and animals.

00:15:40.641 --> 00:15:44.644
More generally, the small RNA field,

00:15:44.645 --> 00:15:47.980
it's thought that there are many sources of

00:15:47.981 --> 00:15:51.551
double-stranded RNAs, one of those sources is

00:15:51.552 --> 00:15:53.219
microRNAs that I started with,

00:15:53.220 --> 00:15:56.055
but there's also viral double-stranded RNAs,

00:15:56.056 --> 00:15:57.523
repeats in the genome,

00:15:57.524 --> 00:16:01.394
they go through this process of making tiny RNAs

00:16:01.395 --> 00:16:04.130
using Dicer and various other components

00:16:04.131 --> 00:16:06.399
and they then regulate gene expression.

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:11.070
They regulate gene expression by a variety of mechanisms,

00:16:11.071 --> 00:16:13.306
the ones I've told you about so far

00:16:13.307 --> 00:16:17.710
are translational repression and RNA degradation,

00:16:17.711 --> 00:16:20.580
which siRNAs mediate by simply causing cleavage.

00:16:20.581 --> 00:16:24.750
More mysterious is the regulation of heterochromatin

00:16:24.751 --> 00:16:26.319
which also involves small RNAs,

00:16:26.320 --> 00:16:30.556
and how that histone modification response

00:16:30.557 --> 00:16:32.458
to small RNAs is a big mystery today.

00:16:32.459 --> 00:16:35.229
Thank you very much.

